data.lsc:8:35  a table's columns hold numbers, text, bools or state options, but thing is an Item.
data.lsc:9:34  a table's keys come from the database, so a table has no key-maker.
data.lsc:13:20  Shop.prices's key (name) is text, so each new row needs it first.
data.lsc:14:19  Shop.prices's rows are found by their name, which is a string. This is an int.
data.lsc:15:20  name is Shop.prices's key, so it can't be changed. Remove the row and add it again with the new name.
data.lsc:16:25  where can use a table's columns with maths, comparisons, &&, ||, not, # and :contains, :startsWith and :endsWith. Work anything else out first, in a variable.
data.lsc:16:31  price is a float, which has no method called text.
data.lsc:17:27  orderBy takes the name of one of Shop.prices's columns: name, price.
data.lsc:18:12  Shop has no table called toys. Its tables are items, people, prices.
data.lsc:19:7  Shop is a database. Use its tables: Shop.items
data.lsc:20:13  remove takes a key: Shop.prices:remove(…). To remove several rows, pick them first: Shop.prices:where(…):remove().
data.lsc:21:25  :has works on the table itself: Shop.prices:has(…). This is a query.
data.lsc:24:5  game holds day, which only the top of the file can see. save it from there, or keep day in an object.
data.lsc:27:19  Item is a class. Save things made from it, like a variable holding one.
